    "markdown": "An on-device AI assistant for Mac needs a working local model as well as a local interface. Start with one typed answer you can check before adding microphone, screen or action permissions. This separates model setup from voice and desktop automation.\n\nThe public HeyPace 0.3.20 preview is text-first. Voice accuracy and hotkey/microphone acceptance remain unverified in its [release acceptance record](https://github.com/HeyPace/pace/releases/tag/v0.3.20). This guide is a suggested evaluation procedure, not a new installed-app test.\n\n## Prepare a minimal local trial\n\nUse a supported Apple Silicon Mac running macOS 26 or later. Follow the [download requirements](/download), open HeyPace and inspect the selected planner in Settings. A local model must be available before an offline trial; downloading a model requires a connection. An explicitly selected external planner changes the data path.\n\nKeep screen reading and optional integrations disabled for this first question. Microphone access is unnecessary when you type. Do not grant unrelated access merely to get a basic answer.\n\n## Ask a known-answer question\n\nType this into the text input:\n\n> Using only this text, list the three items alphabetically: pear, apple, plum. Do not browse, read my screen or take an action.\n\nA successful answer contains apple, pear and plum in that order. Check that no application was opened or document changed. Fluency without the requested ordering is a failed result. Save the app version, selected planner, exact answer and any visible error.\n\nAfter the model is ready, disconnect from the network and repeat with a different list, such as zinc, brass and copper. Record whether an answer completes. A completed offline turn supports that specific text task; it does not establish offline speech, screen analysis, every integration or all background traffic.\n\n## Locate a failure before changing providers\n\n- **No answer online or offline:** check the selected model's readiness and setup instructions. A local menu label does not mean model files are installed or a server is running.\n- **Online works, offline fails:** identify the dependency reported by the app. Record the failure rather than assuming a silent local fallback.\n- **Correct answer, slow startup:** distinguish first model load from a later turn. Do not report a latency advantage from one answer.\n- **A request for new permission or external consent:** inspect why that capability is needed for this text-only task before continuing.\n\nOnce this works, add one capability at a time. Use the [screen-reading trial](/screen-aware-ai-assistant-mac) next, or the [voice-stage diagnostic](/on-device-mac-voice-control-privacy) when you specifically want to evaluate voice. Keep the same known-answer habit as the workflow expands.",
    "html": "<p>An on-device AI assistant for Mac needs a working local model as well as a local interface. Start with one typed answer you can check before adding microphone, screen or action permissions. This separates model setup from voice and desktop automation.</p>\n<p>The public HeyPace 0.3.20 preview is text-first. Voice accuracy and hotkey/microphone acceptance remain unverified in its <a href=\"https://github.com/HeyPace/pace/releases/tag/v0.3.20\">release acceptance record</a>. This guide is a suggested evaluation procedure, not a new installed-app test.</p>\n<h2>Prepare a minimal local trial</h2>\n<p>Use a supported Apple Silicon Mac running macOS 26 or later. Follow the <a href=\"/download\">download requirements</a>, open HeyPace and inspect the selected planner in Settings. A local model must be available before an offline trial; downloading a model requires a connection. An explicitly selected external planner changes the data path.</p>\n<p>Keep screen reading and optional integrations disabled for this first question. Microphone access is unnecessary when you type. Do not grant unrelated access merely to get a basic answer.</p>\n<h2>Ask a known-answer question</h2>\n<p>Type this into the text input:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Using only this text, list the three items alphabetically: pear, apple, plum. Do not browse, read my screen or take an action.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A successful answer contains apple, pear and plum in that order. Check that no application was opened or document changed. Fluency without the requested ordering is a failed result. Save the app version, selected planner, exact answer and any visible error.</p>\n<p>After the model is ready, disconnect from the network and repeat with a different list, such as zinc, brass and copper. Record whether an answer completes. A completed offline turn supports that specific text task; it does not establish offline speech, screen analysis, every integration or all background traffic.</p>\n<h2>Locate a failure before changing providers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No answer online or offline:</strong> check the selected model&#39;s readiness and setup instructions. A local menu label does not mean model files are installed or a server is running.</li>\n<li><strong>Online works, offline fails:</strong> identify the dependency reported by the app. Record the failure rather than assuming a silent local fallback.</li>\n<li><strong>Correct answer, slow startup:</strong> distinguish first model load from a later turn. Do not report a latency advantage from one answer.</li>\n<li><strong>A request for new permission or external consent:</strong> inspect why that capability is needed for this text-only task before continuing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Once this works, add one capability at a time. Use the <a href=\"/screen-aware-ai-assistant-mac\">screen-reading trial</a> next, or the <a href=\"/on-device-mac-voice-control-privacy\">voice-stage diagnostic</a> when you specifically want to evaluate voice. Keep the same known-answer habit as the workflow expands.</p>\n"
